Washington, D.C.  U.S. Senator John McCain (R-AZ), Chairman of the Senate Armed Services Committee, released the following statement on the committees markup of the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2016 and those provisions within it that are of significant importance to the State of Arizona:
I am proud that the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2016 continues to enhance Arizonas remarkable contributions to defending and protecting our nation's security.
Despite the Obama Administrations attempt to retire the A-10 fleet, this bill includes a provision that would prohibit the Air Force from retiring any A-10s and fully fund the flight hours, pilot training, fuel, and operations for all A-10s for Fiscal Year (FY) 2016.The NDAA also includes a provision that would require the Secretary of the Air Force to maintain a minimum of 171 A-10 combat-coded aircraft, many of which are stationed at Davis Monthan Air Force Base (AFB) in Tucson, and directs the Government Accountability Office to conduct a review of the close-air support mission, including the feasibility of transferring the A-10 fleet to the Army and/or Marine Corps. The A-10 is the best close-air support aircraft in our nations arsenal that currently plays an important role in the fight against ISIS in Iraq and Syria, as well as in NATOs efforts to deter Russias aggression in Ukraine. I am proud that the NDAA once again keeps the A-10 flying and prevents any premature retirement of this vital fleet.
In addition, while the Air Forcehad proposed retiring half of the 15-plane EC-130H Compass Call electronic attack fleet stationed at Davis-Monthan AFB, the NDAA includes a provision that would prohibit the use of any funds during FY 2016 to retire, prepare to retire, or place in storage any electronic jamming and surveillance aircraft. As electronic warfare becomes increasingly prevalent on todays battlefields, these planes have protected our air men and women from sophisticated attacks in conflicts across the Middle East such as Libya, Iraq and Afghanistan.
The NDAA also includes provisions that would ensure Arizonas businesses have the ability to continue manufacturing and modernizing weapons systems and defense technologies that enable our military to respond to current and future threats. Further, it includes critical reforms that remove bureaucratic hurdles, increase competition, and save millions in unnecessary spending for Arizona taxpayers. These include provisions that would:
 
Fully fund the Presidents request for 64 Apache helicopters to be remanufactured at Boeing in Mesa;
Increase the Presidents budget request to purchase 100 Tomahawk missiles by $30 million and 49 missiles, which will be manufactured at Raytheon in Tucson;
Help non-traditional defense contracting companies in Arizona by opening fair competition in the defense market and removing unnecessary red tape and bureaucracy; and
Allow the transfer of approximately 125-200 unneeded mobile homes from the Department of Defense to Native American tribes  for which the Navajo and other tribes have long advocated  at no cost to the American taxpayer.
 
Finally, the NDAA includes provisions that would fully fund the Presidents request for important military construction projects in Arizona, including:
 
$18.2 million at Davis Monthan AFB for the Guardian Angel Operations Center;
$16.9 million at Davis Monthan AFB for HC-130J storage and cleaning facilities;
$50.6 million at Marine Corps Air Station Yuma for aircraft maintenance facilities;
$33 million at Luke AFB for a squad operations facility;
$13.2 million at Luke AFB for an aircraft maintenance hangar;
$5.5 million at Luke AFB for a bomb build-up facility;
$5 million at Luke AFB for a fuel offloading facility; and
$3.8 million at Fort Huachuca for communications facility renovations.
 
Importantly, this bill delivers sweeping defense reforms that cut nearly $11 billion in wasteful and excessive spending and reinvest savings in crucial military capabilities. I have long called for an overhaul of our broken acquisition system, and am proud that this bill includes critical measures to strengthen accountability and streamline regulation, as well as modernize and improve the military retirement system, while upholding our commitments to service members, retirees, and their families. These reforms are absolutely necessary given the magnitude of global challenges confronting the men and women serving in our military.
I have always been proud of the many contributions that Arizonas service members and businesses make to our national security. I am confident that through these and other provisions in the NDAA, our state will continue to play an outsized role in protecting and defending the nation. I look forward to working together with my colleagues in the Senate and Chairman Mac Thornberry in the House to produce a defense authorization worthy of the sacrifice of all those who so courageously serve.
